window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][15] = "<ol>\n<li>Did I say what the product is for, for the company and for users?</li>\n<li>Did I choose a segment and a journey step by reach, pain and headroom, and say what I gave up?</li>\n<li>Is the problem specific, with the evidence I have or would collect?</li>\n<li>Did I offer more than one type of move (fix, add, remove, reposition)?</li>\n<li>Did I size the gain with a visible chain, a haircut and a range?</li>\n<li>Did I say who loses, what protects them and how I would see harm?</li>\n<li>Did I check cannibalization, other devices and anything that depends on current behavior?</li>\n<li>Does the metric have a guardrail and a counter metric, and did I say what would make me reverse?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c08-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c08-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C08-01 · The tool-lending marketplace and the unanswered request · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A neighborhood tool-lending marketplace lets people list tools such as drills and ladders and lets neighbors borrow them. In a typical month, borrowers send 40,000 requests (invented), and the team notices that 11,000 loans are completed (invented), which is 27.5 per 100 requests. Management wants more completed loans and proposes to offer damage insurance on every loan.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A product team of three, a fixed quarter, owners who are unpaid volunteers whose goodwill is the scarce resource, and no budget for incentives.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> The funnel for the month (invented): 40,000 requests sent, 34,000 viewed by an owner, 18,000 with a reply, 12,000 accepted, 11,000 completed. In 10 interviews with owners, six say they saw a request and meant to reply later, then forgot; four say they were unsure about the borrower and the return date. Reply timing shows that 62% of replies come within two hours of a push notification, but only 30% of owners have push notifications enabled (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> The loss at each step: 6,000 requests are never viewed (15%), 16,000 viewed requests get no reply (47%), 6,000 replied requests are not accepted, and 1,000 accepted loans are not completed (8%). The largest loss and the most headroom are between viewing and replying. As an observable pain point: &quot;An owner sees a request, cannot tell quickly whether to trust the borrower or when the tool would come back, and postpones replying until the request goes stale.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> Using the selector (FW-C08-03), request-to-view has medium headroom and a cheap fix (push notifications), view-to-reply has the largest loss, and accepted-to-completed has little headroom. The interviews explain the loss: effort (writing a reply) and doubt (who is the borrower and when is the return). Insurance would address damage anxiety, which only some owners raised, and would cost money the team does not have, so it fits the problem poorly. Sizing the chosen step: 34,000 viewed requests, 18,000 replies (52.9%). A fix that lifts replies to 60% gives 20,400, which is 2,400 more.</p>\n<p>Haircut 30%: 1,680. Two thirds of replies become accepted loans (12,000 of 18,000): 1,120 more accepted, and 11 of 12 complete: about 1,030 more completed loans, a rise of 9.3% on 11,000. The alternative, a notification fix, reaches the 6,000 unviewed requests. If it gets a quarter of them viewed (1,500), and those owners reply at a lower rate, say 40%, the result is 600 replies, about 400 accepted loans and 370 completed, which is smaller.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Fix notifications: ask owners to enable push when they list a tool</td>\n<td>Requests viewed</td>\n<td>Cheap; smaller gain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Add one-tap replies (&quot;Yes, pickup after 6&quot;) and show the borrower's history and the return date on the request</td>\n<td>Replies</td>\n<td>About three weeks of work; a risk of low-effort replies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Add damage insurance on every loan</td>\n<td>Trust at handover</td>\n<td>Real money; solves a problem only some owners named</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Do nothing until more data</td>\n<td>Certainty</td>\n<td>A quarter of delay</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B has the largest sized gain and aims at the cause owners described, and it gives up the more thoughtful tone of written replies; owners can still write one. A is cheap and can be done in the same sprint, though its gain is smaller. C may matter later, once replies are no longer the bottleneck.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Build B first, and ship A in the same quarter as a small change. The primary metric is completed loans per 100 requests (baseline 27.5). Guardrails: the share of loans completed (the 91.7% accepted-to-completed rate must not fall) and damage reports per 1,000 loans. Counter metric: the share of one-tap replies that are refusals, which shows whether faster replies are simply faster no's.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario the reply rate rose from 52.9% to 58%, which at the same acceptance and completion rates gives about 12,050 completed loans, and completed loans per 100 requests rose from 27.5 to 30.1 (invented). One-tap refusals made up 22% of replies (invented), which the team treated as healthy because borrowers learned sooner and asked someone else. Damage reports did not change (invented). The team deferred insurance and asked a sample of owners whether it would change their willingness to lend.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If replies had risen but completions had not, the doubt at handover would have been the real barrier, and insurance would have moved up. If one-tap replies had reduced the information exchanged and damage reports had risen, the borrower history would have been shown more prominently. If owners had disliked the change, the legacy-user check (owners who liked writing replies) would have called for keeping a prominent text option. The lesson: find the step with the most loss and headroom, size the options against each other, and take the cheap fix along with the larger move.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c08-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C08-02 · Twitter, 2017: doubling the character limit · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): Twitter's own product blog posts announcing the test (&quot;Giving you more characters to express yourself,&quot; 26 September 2017, read through excerpts) and the rollout (&quot;Tweeting Made Easier,&quot; 7 November 2017, read in full), both primary, the company's own account; contemporaneous news coverage (Fortune, NPR, GeekWire, The Register); a TechCrunch report of company data (30 October 2018); and NPR's 2011 interview with co-founder Biz Stone for the origin of the 140-character limit. Twitter was later renamed X (the company's blog pages now carry the X Corp. name) <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>; this case uses the name in use at the time. The analysis is the author's. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> Twitter had limited posts to 140 characters since its launch in 2006, a limit that co-founder Biz Stone said came from the 160-character length of a text message, with 20 characters left for the username <code>[V: NPR, 2011]</code>. In September 2017 it began testing a 280-character limit with a group of users, for all languages except Chinese, Japanese and Korean. On 7 November 2017 it rolled the longer limit out to nearly all users, with those three languages excluded.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> The brevity of posts was a defining trait of the product, and many users reacted to the test with concern that timelines would fill with long posts, as reported at the time. The company said it wanted to ease the frustration of editing posts to fit while keeping the speed and brevity that defined the product. Some people in the test were on older versions of the apps that could not send longer posts, which the company noted beside its English-only chart of the test <code>[V: Twitter blog, 7 November 2017]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported by the company: in English, about 9% of posts hit the 140-character limit, compared with about 0.4% of posts in Japanese; the same post said that the most common post length was 34 characters in English and 15 in Japanese <code>[V: Twitter blog, 26 September 2017]</code>. In the test group, with the longer limit, only about 1% of English posts hit the cap, only 5% of all posts exceeded 140 characters and only 2% exceeded 190. The company said that people with more room posted more, received more engagement (likes, reposts, mentions), gained more followers and spent more time on the product, and that participants told the company a higher limit made them feel more satisfied with how they expressed themselves, with their ability to find good content and with Twitter overall <code>[V: Twitter blog, 7 November 2017; company-reported, figures not published]</code>. It also said early use of the full limit was high because the feature was new and then settled. A report in October 2018, based on company data, said that about 1% of posts reached the 280-character limit, about 12% were longer than 140 characters, and about 5% exceeded 190 (for English posts; 6% and 3% globally) <code>[V: TechCrunch, 30 October 2018; company data]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> An observable pain point: in English, a person writes a post, finds it over the limit, and spends time cutting it or gives up. The company's 9% figure sizes it: nearly one post in ten hit the cap.</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis.</em> Read through this chapter's frameworks, the decision is a clean use of headroom. The area was the compose step. For English, 9% of posts hitting the cap means a defined group of people losing effort at one step. For Japanese, 0.4% hit the cap, because the language carries more per character, so there was little headroom and the company left it alone. This is a segment choice made by headroom, and the exclusion followed from the data.</p>\n<p>The legacy-user constraint was the brevity that long-time users valued, and the test answered it with a guardrail: the share of posts above 140 characters, which was small. The ecosystem constraint was older apps that could not send longer posts, which made the test group's behavior look more conservative than it would be once all apps supported it. The test also shows a measurement lesson. The 5% figure for the test period was lower than the 12% reported a year after rollout. Possible reasons, which are inference, include users changing their habits over time, a test group that included users on older apps, and a sample that differed from the full user base. In any case a test result is a forecast and not the final outcome, and a guardrail such as &quot;few posts above 140&quot; should be rechecked after launch.</p>\n<p>The company-reported gains in engagement, followers and time spent come from the company's own test and cannot be checked independently from public sources. They were reported as the result of the test, so they say little about long-run growth. A reader should treat them as the company's justification, not as proof of cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a team could have faced; not a record of the company's deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep 140 and change nothing</td>\n<td>The product's identity and consistency</td>\n<td>The friction for the roughly one in ten English posts that hit the cap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Raise to 280 for languages where the cap binds</td>\n<td>Compose friction, with limited change to timelines</td>\n<td>Risk to brevity, which the test could check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Allow very long posts</td>\n<td>Expression</td>\n<td>A larger change to what the product is, and to timelines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Keep 140 and offer a long-form or thread option</td>\n<td>Identity preserved for the short post</td>\n<td>Compose friction stays; a second place to write</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B gives up strict brevity in exchange for removing a measured source of friction, and it is reversible in the sense that the limit can be changed again. C changes the product's character, and A leaves the measured pain. D adds a feature, and adding complexity is the classic reflex that the four-moves framework warns about.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, the company tested 280 for about six weeks and then released it to nearly all languages, leaving Chinese, Japanese and Korean at 140.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: the share of English posts hitting the cap fell from 9% to 1%, and the test showed that most posts stayed short. A follow-up in October 2018, reported by TechCrunch from company data, said that the most common English post length was 33 characters against 34 under the old limit, that about 1% of English posts hit the 280-character limit and that 12% of English posts were longer than 140 characters (6% of posts globally), with findings consistent across seven languages <code>[V: TechCrunch, 30 October 2018]</code>. The company's data did not settle whether the change improved long-run growth; the sources used here do not say.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Had the share of long posts been high in the test, timelines would have been denser and the guardrail would have argued against the change. Had long-time users left in large numbers, the change would have been costly in a way the average hides, and a separate measure of the heaviest users would have shown it. A candidate analyzing this case should also note the survivorship problem: a change that worked is easy to explain afterward, and the same facts would have been read differently if it had failed. The method to carry away: size the pain at the step, find where headroom is small and leave that segment alone, test with a guardrail that protects what users value, and recheck the result after launch.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Seven questions: two Beginner, two Intermediate, three Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code>; every figure is an assumption. Each is written against the six behaviors in section 1 and uses the spine in FW-C08-01. The answers are fuller than the first pass of a real round; a spoken version keeps the same order with fewer words. Where a real product is the setting, the answer reasons from user needs and public information, and states no internal fact.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c08-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C08-01 · Beginner · product design · PS · Google</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you improve the inbox of a widely used email app for people who receive more than 100 emails a day?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists features (smarter search, more labels, a dark mode) without saying who the user is or where the inbox fails them.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States what the product is for, chooses a segment and a step, names a specific problem, offers different types of move and recommends one.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats a wrong automatic decision as the main risk, protects people who rely on the current view, sizes the gain and sets a guardrail on missed important mail.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll assume the existing app on phones and the web, and a goal of making the inbox the place where heavy email users get their work done. I'll note that I know the product only from public information and use.</p>\n<p><em>State.</em> Role: a utility that anchors the user to the company's wider account and services. Users: people with high volume (managers, sales, support, executives), moderate users, and casual users. Funnel: mail arrives, the person triages (decides what needs action), reads, acts, then files or deletes. Metrics I'd expect it to follow: daily use, time in the app, and search use. Constraints: years of mail in people's folders and labels, habits around a chronological view, and the same behavior across devices.</p>\n<p><em>Area.</em> I choose people receiving 100 or more emails a day at the triage step. Their reach is smaller than the whole base, perhaps 5% of users (invented), but the pain is highest, the effort repeats every day and they influence which tool their teams use. I give up casual users, who have little to improve.</p>\n<p><em>Problem.</em> A heavy user cannot tell quickly which messages need a reply from them and which only inform. The workarounds are the evidence: starring, moving mail into folders by hand, and rereading the inbox several times a day. I'd verify by watching three people triage and by looking at how many messages people open and then leave unanswered.</p>\n<p><em>The four moves.</em> Fix: make the existing importance signals more accurate and more visible, since wrong ones teach users to ignore them. Add: a &quot;needs reply&quot; section built from signals such as being addressed directly, a question in the text and the person's past replies to that sender. Remove: take out manual steps in triage, such as having to label a thread to keep track of it. Reposition: treat the inbox as a task list.</p>\n<p><em>Size.</em> If 5% of 500,000,000 users (invented) are heavy, that is 25,000,000 people. If triage takes 40 minutes a day and the section saves 10% of it, that is 4 minutes a day per person; with a 30% haircut for people who ignore the section, about 2.8 minutes, so about 70,000,000 minutes a day across the group, or about 1.2 million hours a day. The number is large because of scale, and I'd still check it with a test, since it assumes people trust the section.</p>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend the Add, with the Fix, in the first version: a &quot;needs reply&quot; section above the normal inbox, never replacing it, with the reason shown (&quot;addressed to you, contains a question&quot;). It addresses the triage problem by giving up a more ambitious reposition. Messages are only highlighted, never hidden, because a wrong decision that buries an important email is the worst failure and the one that destroys trust.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of messages in the section that the user replies to within 24 hours. Input: the share of heavy users who open the section daily. Guardrail: the share of replied-to messages that the section had not flagged, which measures what it misses, and it should stay under 5% (invented). Counter metric: the share of flagged messages that the user dismisses without action, which measures noise.</p>\n<p><em>Risks.</em> Legacy users who rely on the chronological view keep it as the default, with the section optional. Misjudged importance could favor some senders, so the signals are shown and explained, and the user can turn them off. I'd change my mind if watching users showed that triage was fast already and the cost was in writing replies, in which case the lever is drafting help and not sorting.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that improvement prompts at Google can concern its own products at large scale <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or product behavior is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if automatic prioritization hides an important email? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A missed important message costs far more than extra clutter, so I would design to avoid hiding. All mail stays in the inbox; prioritization only adds a section or a marker, never removes mail from the main view by default. Every automatic decision can be undone in one tap, and moving a message into or out of priority teaches the system. I would measure the missed-important rate by user reports and by messages that users move from the low section to priority, with a stop line set in advance, and release first to an opt-in group before changing the default for anyone.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you size the gain and judge whether it is worth it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Through time saved for the heavy segment. A person with 100 emails a day who spends about 8 seconds triaging each uses roughly 13 minutes a day; if prioritization cuts that by 20%, it saves about 2.7 minutes a day. If heavy users are 5% of the base, as I assumed, the average gain is about 0.13 minutes, or 8 seconds, per user a day, and I would also look at satisfaction and retention in the heavy group. The measure is time to first action on priority mail and triage time, not opens, and the guardrail is the missed-important rate. If the saving is below a minute for heavy users, the feature is probably not worth the risk.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c08-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C08-02 · Beginner · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Improve the order-tracking screen of a food-delivery app.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add a map and push notifications,&quot; with no reasoning about the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the customer's problem while waiting, chooses a fix over an addition where it is better, sizes it and gives a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that an honest estimate can hurt conversion at checkout, names the second-order effect and keeps a counter metric on it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The product is a marketplace and its role is revenue and retention: customers who get food when they expect it come back. The segment is customers who have ordered and are waiting. The step is from &quot;order placed&quot; to &quot;delivered,&quot; and the pain is uncertainty: they do not know when the food will arrive or why it is late. The evidence I'd look for is contacts to support asking &quot;where is my order?&quot;, which I'd assume at 6 per 100 orders (invented), and the cost of each contact, assumed at $3 (invented).</p>\n<p><em>Moves.</em> Fix: make the estimated time accurate, and show a range with a reason when something changes (&quot;the restaurant is taking longer&quot;). Add: a live map of the courier. Remove: take away the minute-by-minute countdown that shows precision the app does not have; a countdown that is wrong damages trust more than a range. Reposition: none. I recommend the Fix with the Remove: honest, accurate estimates, which cost less than a map and address the cause of the contacts.</p>\n<p><em>Size.</em> With 1,000,000 orders a month (invented), 6% contact support: 60,000. If the change cuts contacts by 25%, that is 15,000 fewer; with a 30% haircut, 10,500, or about $31,500 a month in support cost. The cost saving is modest, and the larger effect is likely on satisfaction and reordering, which I'd measure and not assume.</p>\n<p><em>Second-order effect.</em> An honest range is longer than an optimistic number, and the estimate shown at checkout affects whether a customer orders at all. If I show &quot;35 to 45 minutes&quot; where the app said &quot;30,&quot; conversion could fall. So the checkout estimate and the tracking estimate must come from the same model, and I'd test checkout conversion as a guardrail.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: support contacts about delivery time per 100 orders. Input: the share of orders delivered within the shown range. Guardrail: conversion at checkout. Counter metric: the share of customers who cancel after seeing a longer estimate, which shows whether honesty is driving them away. I'd change my mind if the data showed that most contacts were about missing items rather than timing, in which case the lever is order accuracy.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> An honest delivery estimate can lower conversion at checkout. How do you handle it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would test it and compare the costs. Suppose showing a longer, honest estimate reduces checkout conversion by 1%, and a typical order earns $4 of margin, so the loss is about $0.04 per checkout attempt. If it cuts where-is-my-order contacts from 6 to 4 per 100 orders, at $3 each that saves about $0.06 per order, and it should raise repeat orders because the food arrives when expected. So the decision rests on the 30-day reorder rate and contacts together. I would run the test for four weeks, and ship if net contribution per customer is positive and complaints about lateness fall.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What do you show when an order is late? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A proactive message with a new estimate and a short reason, before the customer has to ask. Beyond a stated threshold, such as 15 minutes late, offer a choice: keep waiting, cancel with a full refund, or accept a credit. I would measure contacts per 100 orders, refund and credit cost, and how often the estimate changes, because an estimate that keeps shifting damages trust more than one that is wrong once. The guardrail is that the credit rate stays within a budget set with finance.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c08-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C08-03 · Intermediate · product design · PS · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Improve a notes app that syncs across phone, tablet and laptop.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add tags, a better editor and handwriting support,&quot; with no reasoning about the cross-device experience.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Focuses on the cross-device problem, names legacy users and consistency as constraints, and chooses a fix over a feature.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats trust as the product, chooses the metric that matches the failure (lost content, not engagement), and handles encryption, older versions and offline editing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll assume the notes app that comes with the user's devices, and I know it only from public information and use. Role: a utility that makes each device more useful and keeps users within the same set of devices; its value grows with every device a person adds, so cross-device behavior is central. Users: heavy note-takers (students and professionals with thousands of notes), casual users (lists and reminders) and people who share notes. Funnel: capture, organize, find, revisit on another device, share. Constraints: years of notes and folder structures, consistent behavior across every device, offline editing, encrypted notes, and devices running older versions.</p>\n<p><em>Area.</em> I choose heavy note-takers at the moment of opening a note on a second device. Their stakes are the highest because their notes are their work, and the step has a specific failure: a note edited on one device appears stale or differs on another, or two edits clash and one is lost. I give up casual users, who rarely feel it. I'd verify by looking at how often a note changes on two devices within a short time and what users do after a conflict.</p>\n<p><em>Problem.</em> &quot;I edited a note on my phone, opened it on my laptop and saw the old version, or lost a paragraph.&quot; The workarounds are the evidence: copying text into a message to myself, saving a second copy, or emailing notes to myself. These show a loss of trust, which for a notes app is the heart of the product.</p>\n<p><em>Moves.</em> Fix: show a clear sync state (&quot;up to date&quot; or &quot;syncing&quot;) and handle a conflict by keeping both versions and not by overwriting. Add: a version history, so any loss can be undone. Remove: nothing. Reposition: none. I recommend the Fix and a small Add: visible sync state, a keep-both rule for conflicts, and a short version history.</p>\n<p><em>Constraint scan.</em> Legacy users: existing notes and folders must be untouched, and no change to how a note is stored should require action. Ecosystem: the behavior must work the same on every device, so I'd stage the rollout across all of them, and on devices on older versions the new behavior degrades gracefully, with no loss. Privacy: for encrypted notes the conflict is resolved on the devices, and the service never needs to read the note.</p>\n<p><em>Size.</em> Assume 20,000,000 people use two or more devices (invented). If 4% hit a conflict or a stale view in a month, that is 800,000; if 10% of those lose content, 80,000. A keep-both rule that prevents 80% of losses prevents 64,000 losses a month. The count is small against the base, and the severity is high: one lost note can end a person's use of the app. So the size does not say &quot;skip it&quot;; it says to measure the right thing.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: lost-content incidents per 100,000 multi-device users per month, from reports and detected overwrites, with a target near zero. Input: the share of conflicts resolved by keeping both versions. Guardrails: sync delay at the 95th percentile, and storage used by duplicate versions. Counter metric: complaints about duplicate notes, since keep-both can clutter a library. I'd change my mind if the data showed that conflicts were rare and the common complaint was finding notes, in which case the area is search.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that improvement prompts at Apple concern its own devices and weigh consistency across them <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about how any existing notes product works.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle a note edited offline on two devices? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The rule is never lose content. When the same note changed on both devices, the app merges non-overlapping changes automatically, and where changes collide it keeps both versions and shows a conflict copy that the user can reconcile. A version history of about 30 days lets users recover an earlier version. The main metric is lost-content incidents, with a target of zero and every incident investigated; secondary measures are conflicts per 1,000 syncs and the time to reconcile. I would test with deliberate offline edits on several devices before release.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would encryption and older versions affect your plan? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;End-to-end encryption means the server cannot read notes, so features such as server-side search, merging by content and recovery depend on keys the user holds. I would run search on the device, keep version history encrypted with the same protection, and be clear about recovery: if a user loses their key, the content may be unrecoverable, so I would offer a recovery option and explain the tradeoff plainly. The guardrails are lock-out incidents and the share of users who cannot find an old note. Older app versions need a migration path so that no note is stranded.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c08-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C08-04 · Intermediate · product design · PS · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Improve the walking experience in a maps app.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add augmented-reality directions and more landmarks.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments walkers, chooses a moment in the walking journey, names the problem, offers different moves and recommends one with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the hardware (a watch, haptics) to keep the phone in a pocket, addresses distracted walking and battery, and protects users who rely on audio or who drive.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll assume the built-in maps app on phones and watches. Role: a utility that makes the devices more useful, with walking as one of several modes alongside driving and transit. Users: commuters walking to transit, visitors in an unfamiliar area, and errand walkers. I'll organize the walking journey as plan, start, move, arrive.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Pain or loss</th>\n<th>Headroom</th>\n<th>Choice</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Plan the route</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Rejected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Start walking: which way do I go?</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Medium to high: the person does not know which direction to walk</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Chosen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Move: follow the turns</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Second</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Arrive: find the right entrance</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Rejected for now</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Problem.</em> At the start of a walk, the person standing on a street corner cannot tell which way to go. The map dot may not show the direction they face, so they walk a few steps, watch the dot, and correct. The workaround is the evidence (walking the wrong way for half a minute). Visitors in an unfamiliar area feel it most, since they have no sense of direction to lean on.</p>\n<p><em>Moves.</em> Fix: improve the accuracy of the facing direction and give a plain first instruction (&quot;walk toward the park, on Oak Street&quot;). Add: turn cues on the watch, a tap for left and a different tap for right, so the person need not look at the phone, which also helps in the rain and in a crowd. Remove: reduce the map's detail while walking, since clutter makes the route harder to see. Reposition: none. I recommend the Fix with the watch cues. They use what the devices are good at (sensing direction, haptics on the wrist) and they address the start.</p>\n<p><em>Size.</em> Assume 50,000,000 navigation sessions a week (invented), 8% on foot: 4,000,000. If 15% begin in the wrong direction, that is 600,000; the change cuts it by 40%: 240,000; with a 30% haircut, 168,000 avoided wrong starts. At 1.5 minutes saved each, that is 252,000 minutes, or about 4,200 hours a week. The gain is in time and confidence, and I'd say the value is the experience and not a number tied to revenue.</p>\n<p><em>Constraints and risks.</em> Legacy users: drivers and transit riders must see no change. Safety: the point of the haptic cues is to reduce screen-watching while walking, and I'd test that screen-on time falls. Accessibility: people who use audio guidance should get the first instruction spoken as well. Battery: continuous direction sensing costs power, so it runs only while navigating. Privacy: the facing direction is computed on the device.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of walking routes whose first 30 seconds follow the route direction, inferred from location. Input: the share of walkers using watch cues. Guardrails: battery use per walking hour, and screen-on time while walking, which should fall. Counter metric: the share of users who turn the cues off, since cues that annoy will be disabled. I'd change my mind if the data showed that wrong starts were rare and the real loss was at the arrival (the wrong entrance), in which case I'd move to that step.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that improvement prompts at Apple concern its devices and the experience across them <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about how any existing maps product works.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you keep people from staring at the phone while they walk? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By moving guidance off the screen. Haptic taps on a watch for left and right turns, short audio prompts through earbuds, and a simple lock-screen view reduce the need to look. I would measure the share of walking time with the screen on and the number of times a user stops to check, with safety reports as a guardrail. I would make audio guidance work well with screen readers and for people who rely on audio, and keep the visual map for those who want it, since a feature that helps most users and breaks for blind users is not a good design.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you protect battery life? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By treating battery as a requirement with a budget. Use lower-power location updates when the route is straight, and wake the screen only at decisions. I would measure battery drain per 30-minute walk against a baseline, with a target of no more than about 3 percentage points more than a walk without guidance, and test on older devices. If a mode costs more, I would say so in the interface and let the user choose. The guardrail is the rate of users who turn off guidance mid-walk because the battery is low.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c08-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C08-05 · Hard · product design · PS · Meta</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Many users of a social app say they spend more time on it than they want. How would you improve the product?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add a screen-time reminder,&quot; with no view of the conflict with the business or of who is affected.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the tension between time spent and user wellbeing, chooses a segment, offers different moves, and sets metrics that allow time spent to fall.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Agrees in advance how much engagement the company will trade, protects younger users with defaults, applies Goodhart's law, and says what would change the plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll start with the tension, since it shapes the answer. I'll assume the product earns revenue from advertising, so time spent matters commercially (an assumption I'd confirm), and that users are telling us the product sometimes works against their own goals. That is a goal conflict, and the interviewer will want to see how I handle it, not how I avoid it.</p>\n<p><em>State.</em> Role: a core product and revenue engine. Users: people who say they spend more than they want, which I'd define by a short survey and by behavior (late-night sessions, long unplanned sessions), not only by volume; people who spend a lot and are content; and people who use it briefly. Funnel: open, scroll, interact, close. A feed that does not end has no natural stopping point.</p>\n<p><em>Area.</em> I choose adult users who report that they spend more time than they want, at the moment of a long unplanned session. Their stated regret is a direct signal, their number is large, and the product's design contributes to the problem. I treat younger users separately with stricter defaults, because they are more vulnerable, and I'd involve safety and legal teams from the start; rules differ by country <code>[V: rules differ by country, for example India's DPDP Act treats anyone under 18 as a child]</code>.</p>\n<p><em>Problem.</em> The feed has no natural end. People do not notice time passing, and the product offers no moment to decide to stop. Workarounds: deleting the app and reinstalling it, using the phone's own limits, and logging out.</p>\n<p><em>Moves.</em> Fix: make the existing time tools easier to find and use. Add: a &quot;you're all caught up&quot; point after new content ends, and an optional reminder after a session length the person chooses. Remove: the next-item autoplay after the caught-up point. Reposition: from maximizing time to maximizing value, a change in what the product optimizes. Public accounts of one such shift exist: in early 2018 Facebook's chief executive said a change to the News Feed would reduce time spent in favor of more meaningful interactions, and the company reported a drop in time spent <code>Public</code> <code>[V: CS-C05-02, Facebook's January 2018 post and call]</code> (C05, CS-C05-02). I recommend the Add and the Fix first, with the Reposition as the longer question.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics, and the trade.</em> Primary: the share of surveyed users in the segment who say they are satisfied with how they spend time on the product. That is a self-report and a proxy, and I'd pair it with behavior. Guardrails: long-run retention (a product that is healthier to use should keep people over years) and the reach of creators, who depend on attention. Counter metric: time spent and sessions per user, which I expect to fall, and I'd agree before launch how much of a fall is acceptable (say no more than 3% in the first quarter, invented), so that the team and leadership decide the trade in advance and not under pressure. Goodhart's law applies: if satisfaction became a target, it could be raised by changing how the survey is asked, so I'd keep the question fixed and audit it.</p>\n<p><em>Risks.</em> Winners and losers: users in the segment gain; creators who depend on long sessions lose reach; advertisers see fewer impressions. Defaults have a large effect on what people end up with (<code>MM-defaults</code>, C07), so for younger users the reminders and limits are on by default. I'd reverse the break point if retention among the segment fell without a gain in reported satisfaction. I'd change my plan if the survey showed that the regret came mostly from one feature, and a targeted change would do more than a general break.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that improvement prompts at Meta can pair engagement questions with wellbeing and integrity tradeoffs <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or internal decision is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How much engagement are you willing to trade, and who decides? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;This must be decided before the work, with leadership, in writing. For example, accept up to a 2% fall in daily time among users who opt into limits if satisfaction among them rises by a stated amount, and set protective defaults for younger users even at a larger cost. The decision record names the trade, the measure and the date it will be reviewed. Without it, teams drift back to the number the company rewards. Goodhart's law applies in both directions: if we target a well-being score, it can be gamed, so I would keep several measures.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you tell whether the changes help users and not just reduce time? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would look at what the lost time was and what users say. Compare time well spent from a short survey item, 90-day retention, and active interactions for users who got the changes against a holdout. If time drops and satisfaction rises with retention flat, the change helped. If time drops and satisfaction does not move, I only reduced usage. I would also check the users who opted in, since they differ from others, and avoid reading the effect from them alone.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c08-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C08-06 · Hard · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> An enterprise software product has a legacy report feature used by 4% of customers that consumes 20% of the team's maintenance effort. The team wants to remove it. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Remove it, since few customers use it,&quot; or &quot;keep it, since customers pay for it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks who the 4% are and what they pay, applies the fence test, compares options and sets a migration path.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies revenue at risk with and without a path, chooses a path that gives customers time and a replacement, defines how success and harm will be measured, and says when to stop.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd treat this as a removal decision with legacy users, and the number 4% is the start of the questions, not the answer. First the fence test (FW-C08-05): who is in the 4%, what do they pay, why do they use it, and why was it built? A small share of customers can be a large share of revenue, and enterprise customers often build routines around a report.</p>\n<p><em>Assumptions.</em> 5,000 customers (invented), so 200 use the feature. They average $30,000 a year (invented), so $6,000,000 a year is at stake. The 20% of maintenance effort equals two engineer-years on a team of ten (invented), at $200,000 each fully loaded, so $400,000 a year.</p>\n<p><em>Options.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Revenue at risk</th>\n<th>Cost and effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Remove it now</td>\n<td>15% churn among the 200 (invented): $900,000</td>\n<td>Frees $400,000 a year; breaks routines; damages trust</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Keep it and freeze it</td>\n<td>Near zero</td>\n<td>Saves part of the effort; the debt stays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Replace it with a better report and migrate users over 12 months</td>\n<td>3% churn (invented): $180,000</td>\n<td>Frees $400,000 a year after the transition; costs migration work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Keep it as it is</td>\n<td>Zero</td>\n<td>Keeps all the cost and the opportunity cost of two engineers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The arithmetic: 15% of $6,000,000 is $900,000, more than the $400,000 saved, so an abrupt removal loses money in the first year. With a path, 3% of $6,000,000 is $180,000, less than the saving, and the saving then continues.</p>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend C: build the replacement, announce the change early, migrate the data and reports automatically, keep the old feature running for 12 months, and then remove it. It gives up the immediate saving. First I'd talk to a sample of the 200, especially the largest, to learn why they use it, and the replacement should meet that need and not just the feature's description. I'd check contracts and any commitment that names the feature.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of the 200 accounts migrated by the deadline. Guardrails: churn and contraction among the 200 compared with other accounts, and support tickets. Success: maintenance effort actually freed, and what it is used for; if it goes to the roadmap, I'd say what shipped. Counter metric: sentiment among affected accounts.</p>\n<p><em>Risks and stopping rule.</em> The loudest accounts may be the strategic ones, so I'd treat each large account individually. I'd stop the removal, or extend the date, if migration is below a threshold set in advance (say 70% by month nine, invented) or if a strategic account says it will leave. I'd change the plan entirely if the interviews showed the feature does something nothing else does, in which case the product is missing a capability, and the question becomes how to build it well.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What do you do for the customers who rely on the report? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Give them a path and time. Build a replacement that covers most of what the report does (say 80% of the use cases), provide an export for the rest, announce the removal 12 months ahead, and give the largest accounts a named contact and help to migrate. For the few customers whose needs cannot be met, offer a paid support arrangement for a limited time. I would track migration progress by account, and tell customers early, with dates, what will change and what will not.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you decide when to stop the removal? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With triggers set before the announcement. For example, pause if more than 3 of the 200 affected accounts cancel because of the change, if support tickets about the report exceed a set level, or if accounts representing more than 25% of the affected revenue have not migrated by month nine. When a trigger is hit, extend the date and fix the cause: a missing capability, a migration tool, a pricing issue. The aim is to reduce maintenance cost without losing the revenue that the removal was supposed to protect.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c08-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C08-07 · Hard · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A mature productivity app has had flat weekly active users for six quarters, although the team has shipped many winning experiments. What would you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Run more experiments and add more features.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Questions why wins do not add up, looks at cohorts and segments, and considers larger moves.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Reconciles the sum of experiment lifts with the actual outcome, names the local-maximum risk, tests a new segment or job with a cheap bet, and sets expectations for the first quarter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd start with the puzzle in the prompt: many winning experiments and a flat result. They cannot both be fully true, so something is wrong in how the wins are counted, or the wins are being offset.</p>\n<p><em>Reconcile first.</em> Suppose the team's experiments claim an added 9% of weekly actives over six quarters (invented), and the actual change is zero. I'd list the usual reasons. Novelty: lifts fade after a few weeks, and a test measured over two weeks overstates them. Overlap: two wins can improve the same users and not add. Shifts: a win in one place can reduce use elsewhere (cannibalization within the product).</p>\n<p>Proxies: experiments may be won on a metric (clicks on a feature) that is a <a href=\"#gl-proxy-metric\">proxy metric</a> for value and is not the number that matters. Offsets: other things are pulling the number down, such as older cohorts decaying faster or new-user cohorts getting smaller. I'd check each with data, using cohorts (C05): are older cohorts retaining as before, and are newer cohorts arriving at the same rate and activating as well? The experiment mechanics are C14.</p>\n<p><em>What the answer is likely to be.</em> If the flat number is a balance between decay and acquisition, the improvements are keeping the line level and the real question is growth. If new cohorts are healthy and older ones decaying, the problem is long-term retention. If new cohorts are small, the product may be saturating its market. Each leads to a different move.</p>\n<p><em>Moves.</em> Fix: remove what is hurting retention for older cohorts (for example, complexity added over six quarters). Add: a capability for a job the product does not serve today. Remove: features that few use and that make the product harder to learn. Reposition: a different segment or job. The local-maximum risk is real: a team that optimizes steps one at a time will not find a better position, so alongside the steady work I'd run one or two larger, cheap bets, such as interviews and a prototype for an adjacent segment, with a stated criterion for success, and I'd protect them from the weekly metric.</p>\n<p><em>Expectations.</em> I'd tell leadership that the first quarter should produce a diagnosis and a small number of bets, not growth, and I'd agree what we would look at: the reconciled lift, cohort curves, and the results of the bets. The primary metric stays weekly actives for the core segment, with retention by cohort as an input, and a guardrail on the health of the core experience (task completion, support load). A counter metric is complexity: the number of features a new user must learn.</p>\n<p><em>What would change my mind.</em> If reconciliation showed that the experiment wins were real and the offset was a one-off event such as a change in how users are counted, the answer would be a data fix and not a product move. If the larger bets failed on their criterion, I'd concentrate the team on retention for older cohorts.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you reconcile many winning experiments with flat weekly actives? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would find where the wins go. Common reasons are novelty (lifts fade after the test window), overlap (two wins that move the same users and do not add), measuring a metric among engaged users who were already active, and shifts in the definition of an active user over six quarters. The most reliable check is a cumulative holdout: keep 1% of users on the product as it was a year ago and compare their weekly activity with everyone else's. If the gap is much smaller than the sum of the lifts, the claimed wins were overstated. I would also check seasonality and acquisition changes.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What bet would you make outside the local maximum? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A small test of a new job or segment, for example shared workspaces for teams of three to five. Offer it to a few thousand users, measure invites sent, invitees who become active, and retention of invited users after four weeks, with cost of the team as small and time boxed to a quarter. I would set the expectation with leadership that the first quarter is for learning and may not move weekly actives, and agree the kill criteria in advance. A bet like this tests whether there is growth outside the current use.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c08-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c08-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C08-01 · What is this product for?</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Assign a role (revenue engine, growth engine, retention anchor, strategic bet, utility) to each of these, and say what &quot;an improvement&quot; would mean for each: (a) the built-in calculator on a phone; (b) a free email service from a company that sells advertising; (c) a paid project-management tool sold to companies; (d) a video app launched to hold a position against a rival; (e) a free app that gets people into a company's paid app.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are starting an improvement answer and must state the role.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None beyond the descriptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Two sentences each.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the role line of the state card (FW-C08-02), and state the role as an assumption.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Utility: an improvement is accuracy, speed and rarely-surprising behavior, even if time spent falls. (b) Retention anchor or growth engine, with advertising as revenue: improve what keeps people returning and brings their other activity into the account, and watch that changes do not cost trust. (c) Revenue engine: improve what drives renewals and expansion, for the buyer as well as the daily user. (d) Strategic bet: an improvement is progress on the position (reach, quality versus the rival), accepting lower short-term returns. (e) Growth engine: improve the conversion of free users to the paid app, and watch for a change that raises sign-ups and lowers quality.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Some roles overlap; choose the primary one and name the second, as in (b).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Naming a role commits you to a view of what matters; ask the interviewer to correct it, since a wrong role changes the whole answer.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c08-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C08-02 · Headroom, then downstream value</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A shopping app's funnel for a month: 100,000 people view a product page; 60,000 add to cart; 30,000 start checkout; 24,000 pay. Benchmark rates for comparable apps: add to cart 70%, checkout start 60%, payment 95%. Which step should you improve to add the most paid orders?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The goal is more paid orders. You have one quarter.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Current rates: view to add 60%; add to checkout start 50%; checkout start to pay 80%.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Assume newly won users convert at the current downstream rates.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Compute headroom in users at each step, then follow those users downstream to paid orders (FW-C08-03, FW-C08-06).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Step 1 (view to add): gap = (70% − 60%) × 100,000 = 10,000 users; downstream conversion is 50% × 80% = 40%: 4,000 paid orders. Step 2 (add to checkout start): gap = (60% − 50%) × 60,000 = 6,000 users; downstream 80%: 4,800 paid orders. Step 3 (checkout start to pay): gap = (95% − 80%) × 30,000 = 4,500 users; downstream 100%: 4,500 paid orders. The checkout start has the most downstream value, 4,800, though the first step has the largest gap in users. Apply a haircut, since marginal users convert less than average, and check the pain at step 2 with data before choosing.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Choose step 1 if it is far cheaper to improve, and say that the difference between 4,000 and 4,800 is within the uncertainty of the assumptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Benchmarks are rough and the downstream-rate assumption is generous; the point is that the largest raw gap is not automatically the best step.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c08-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C08-03 · Fill in the selector</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A language-learning app wants more people to complete their first week. Rate these candidate areas on reach, pain or loss, headroom and fit, and choose one: (a) new users at sign-up; (b) new users at the first lesson; (c) users on days 3 to 5, when streaks break; (d) users choosing a plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You have no data beyond what is below.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Invented figures. Sign-up: 90% of arrivals complete it. First lesson: 70% of sign-ups start it. Days 3 to 5: 40% of those who started a lesson miss a day and 55% of those do not return. Plan choice: 12% choose a paid plan, which is typical.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the selector table (FW-C08-03) and name the rejected rows.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Reach high, loss low (90% complete), headroom low: reject. (b) Reach high, loss medium (30% do not start), headroom medium; fit medium. (c) Reach medium (people who started), loss high (of the 40% who miss a day, 55% leave, so 22% of starters), headroom high; fit high for &quot;complete the first week.&quot; (d) Reach low, loss not relevant to the goal: reject. Choose (c), the break in the first week. Name (b) as second, and say that the first lesson step is worth a check because a person who never starts never reaches days 3 to 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Choose (b) first if the data show that most of the lost people never started a lesson, since the largest absolute loss is there: 30% of sign-ups at (b) against 22% of starters at (c), and the base at (b) is larger.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> (c) is the better fit for the goal and (b) may have more users to win; the two differ by what is known, so say which assumption would flip the choice.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c08-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C08-04 · Size it</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A recipe app has 500,000 weekly users. 20% reach a &quot;create a shopping list&quot; step, where 50% complete it today. A fix is expected to raise completion to 55%. Each completed list is worth $2 in revenue (invented). Use a 40% haircut. What is the weekly and annual value, and what is the payback if the change costs $150,000 in team time?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The team has two candidate projects and needs to compare them.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Show each step.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the sizing ladder (FW-C08-06).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Reach: 500,000 × 0.20 = 100,000. At 50%: 50,000 complete. At 55%: 55,000, so 5,000 more. Haircut 40%: 5,000 × 0.6 = 3,000. Value: 3,000 × $2 = $6,000 a week; × 52 = $312,000 a year. Payback: $150,000 ÷ $6,000 = 25 weeks. Check by a second route: a five-point lift on a base of 100,000 is 5,000, and a 40% haircut leaves 3,000, the same. Say the range: if the haircut were 60%, the weekly value would be $4,000 and the payback 37.5 weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> If the $2 value comes from a correlation (people who complete lists spend more), credit part of it: at half, $3,000 a week and a payback of 50 weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A longer payback is not a reason to decline automatically; compare it with the other project's payback and with strategic value.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c08-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C08-05 · Four moves</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Problem: in a photo-printing app, customers abandon the &quot;choose a size&quot; step because there are 14 sizes and the differences are unclear. List a fix, an add, a remove and a reposition, and recommend one.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The app is mature and the step has high traffic.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> 38% of customers who reach the step leave (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> One line per move.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use FW-C08-05, and test any removal with the fence questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Fix: label each size with what it is good for (&quot;fits a standard frame&quot;) and preselect the most common. Add: a size recommender that asks where the print will go. Remove: cut the 14 sizes to six, keeping those that account for most orders (check that some rare sizes are not needed by a segment such as professional photographers). Reposition: sell &quot;wall displays&quot; and not &quot;prints,&quot; with a few curated sets. Recommend the Fix with a trial of the Remove for the least-ordered sizes, because they are cheap, address the cause (too many unclear options) and are easy to reverse.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Recommend the Remove alone if the order data show that eight of the 14 sizes are under 3% of orders each (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Removing choices simplifies the step and may lose customers who need a rare size; keep a &quot;more sizes&quot; route for them.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c08-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C08-06 · Fence test</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A team proposes removing: (a) a rarely used keyboard shortcut; (b) a confirmation prompt before deleting an account; (c) an export-to-fax option in a business tool. For each, write the questions you would ask before removing it, and your recommendation.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The product is mature and the team wants to simplify.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> (a) used by 0.5% of sessions; (b) skipped by 98% of users, who click through; (c) used by 0.2% of customers, mostly large.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Two to three questions each.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Apply the fence test: who uses it, why was it built, what happens to them, how would you know it was a mistake.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Who uses it, and are they heavy users who value speed? Low risk; remove or move it behind a setting, and measure the heavy users' task time. (b) Why was it built? It guards against an irreversible mistake; 98% click-through shows it annoys most users and protects a few. Do not remove it; make it smarter (require it only for accounts with data, and allow a short undo period). (c) Who are the 0.2%, and what do they pay? Large customers in sectors that still use fax may be high-value and slow to change. Keep it or offer a replacement and a long notice.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> For (b), keep the prompt and test a lighter version, measuring accidental deletions.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Every prompt costs the many to protect the few; the right answer depends on how bad the mistake is and whether it can be undone.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c08-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C08-07 · Winners and losers</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A note-taking app plans to move its &quot;new note&quot; button from the bottom right to the top left to match a design change on another platform. Fill in a winners-and-losers table.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Heavy users have used the current position for years.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> 70% of new notes come from 10% of users (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Four rows.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use FW-C08-07 and name the measure for each row.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Rows: (1) heavy users, who lose a habit; reaction: mis-taps and irritation; mitigation: a setting to keep the old position for six months and a short notice; measure: time to create a note and the rate of accidental taps. (2) New users, who gain consistency with other apps they use; measure: first-note completion. (3) Left-handed users, who may gain or lose; measure: task time by handedness setting. (4) Users on other devices, who lose consistency unless the change is made everywhere; mitigation: ship on all devices together. Because 70% of notes come from 10% of users, the average could look neutral while the main users are worse off, so measure heavy users separately.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Do not make the change, and say that consistency with another platform is a weak reason against the habit of the people who create most of the content.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A choice that is neutral on average and harmful to the core is a decision to make knowingly, not by default.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c08-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C08-08 · Gross versus net</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A subscription app has 100,000 subscribers at $10 a month. It adds a $5 plan with ads. After launch, 40,000 people are on the $5 plan: 15,000 downgraded from the $10 plan and 25,000 are new. What are the monthly revenues before and after, how large is the net gain, and how much does the gross figure overstate it? At what share of existing subscribers choosing the new plan does the net gain become zero?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The team plans to report the new plan's revenue as growth.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Show the arithmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Count net, not gross (FW-C08-04, cannibalization).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Before: 100,000 × $10 = $1,000,000. After: 85,000 × $10 = $850,000, plus 40,000 × $5 = $200,000, total $1,050,000. Net gain $50,000 a month. Gross revenue from the new plan is $200,000, four times the net gain. Break-even: if d of the 40,000 come from existing subscribers, the net is $5 × (40,000 − d) − $5 × d = $5 × (40,000 − 2d), which is zero at d = 20,000, so at 50% cannibalization. At 37.5% (15,000 of 40,000) the plan is still positive. Add the ad revenue from the $5 users and the possibility that some later upgrade, which would improve the picture.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compare with a holdout: offer the plan to a random half of new visitors only, so that downgrades from existing users can be seen separately.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A cheaper plan can widen reach and reduce revenue per user; the net, not the gross, is the number to report.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c08-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C08-09 · A timed full run</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Give a 25-minute answer to: &quot;How would you improve a public-transport app for people who ride every day?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Self-scored; record yourself. Assume a city, a platform and a goal, and say them aloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None. Invent only what you label.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Use the short-version budget in section 4: one segment, one step, two moves, a one-line size, one metric, one loser.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the full spine (FW-C08-01) and score yourself against the six behaviors in section 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> An outline of a strong answer. Clarify and role: a utility and retention anchor for a wider mobility offering; the goal is that regular riders plan and travel with the app. State: users are daily commuters, occasional riders and visitors; funnel is plan, travel, disruption, repeat. Area: daily commuters at disruptions: reach high, pain high when a delay happens, headroom high. Problem: they learn about a disruption late.</p>\n<p>Moves: Fix (accuracy and timing of delay alerts); Add (a &quot;leave by&quot; alert that updates). Size: commuters × trips with delay × share who act. Metric: on-time arrival among alert users against a holdout. Loser: users who dislike alerts, or who rely on the timetable view; keep it. Reverse if the false-alarm rate is above a threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Choose visitors at the first trip (how to pay and where to board) and treat commuters as served.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The commuter choice has more repeat value; the visitor choice may have more headroom per person; state the criterion.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c08-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C08-10 · Handling interventions</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a one- to two-sentence reply to each interviewer intervention: (a) &quot;What is this product for?&quot; (b) &quot;Why that part of the product?&quot; (c) &quot;How big could this be?&quot; (d) &quot;What about the people who like it as it is?&quot; (e) &quot;Wouldn't this just move usage from somewhere else?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are partway through an answer about improving a video-calling app.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Use any assumptions you state.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Two minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the table of interventions in section 4: each points to a missing step.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) &quot;For users, it is the easiest way to talk face to face with people who are far away; for the company, I assume it is a retention anchor for the wider account. Tell me if that is off.&quot; (b) &quot;Call setup is where most failed calls start; it reaches nearly every user, and I'd expect it to have more headroom than the in-call controls.&quot; (c) &quot;If 10% of calls fail at setup and I halve that, with a 30% haircut, I'd expect about 3.5% more successful calls, which is large at this scale.&quot; (d) &quot;Regular users have habits around the current setup. I'd keep the old path for a period and measure their completion time separately.&quot; (e) &quot;A little: some calls would have succeeded on a second try. I'd count net successful calls against a holdout, not the gross number.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Record yourself, and check that each reply takes under 20 seconds and returns to the spine.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Short replies keep momentum and can skip nuance; offer more if asked.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for four weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: role and state.</em> Take a product you use (IQ-C08-01, IQ-C08-02, EX-C08-01) and write its five-line state card in five minutes. Say the role, the users, the funnel, the metrics and the constraint aloud.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: area and size.</em> Use IQ-C08-03, IQ-C08-04, EX-C08-02 or EX-C08-03. Build the selector table and choose with a criterion; then size the move with a chain and a range (EX-C08-04). Twelve minutes.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: moves and losers.</em> Use IQ-C08-05, IQ-C08-06, EX-C08-05, EX-C08-06 or EX-C08-07. Offer a fix, an add, a remove and a reposition, test any removal, and fill in the winners-and-losers table. Fifteen minutes.</li>\n<li><em>Week 4: full rounds.</em> Run two 45-minute answers and one 25-minute answer (EX-C08-09) on products you have not prepared, with a partner who uses the interventions in section 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to interrupt with &quot;What is this product for?&quot; and &quot;What about the people who like it as it is?&quot; at random points, and to ask once &quot;Wouldn't this just move usage from somewhere else?&quot; Practice answering in under a minute.</p>\n<p><strong>Record and review.</strong> Record every full run. Listen once for structure and once for wording. Count how many moves were additions, whether you named a loser, and whether you gave a range for the size.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I state the product's role and the users before proposing anything?</li>\n<li>Did I choose a segment and a step by reach, pain and headroom, and say what I gave up?</li>\n<li>Did I describe a specific problem with evidence or a way to get it?</li>\n<li>Did I offer more than one type of move, including a fix or a removal?</li>\n<li>Did I size the gain with a visible chain, a haircut and a range?</li>\n<li>Did I name who loses and how I would protect them?</li>\n<li>Did I check cannibalization, other devices and dependence on current behavior?</li>\n<li>Did the metric have a guardrail and a counter metric?</li>\n<li>Did I say what would make me reverse or change the plan?</li>\n<li>Did I finish with the two-sentence close from section 4?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the product, the role, the area, the move chosen, your size and range, your score out of ten and the step you rushed. After eight drills, look for the step you skip most (usually the losers or the size) and drill that step alone for a week.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Write the state card (FW-C08-02) for the product or area you work on: role, users, funnel, metrics, constraints. Check each line against data you can see and mark which lines are assumptions. Share it with your manager and ask what is wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Build the selector (FW-C08-03) for your area: three segments and the steps for each, with reach, loss, headroom and fit from real data. Choose where the next effort would be most valuable and compare with what the team is working on. Note any gap and discuss it.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> For one planned change, fill in the constraint scan (FW-C08-04) and the winners-and-losers table (FW-C08-07). Ask an engineer who knows the history what depends on the current behavior and ask support what customers say about it.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Size one proposed change with the ladder (FW-C08-06): write each assumption, apply a haircut, give a range, and check by a second route. Compare with the actual result of a similar past change.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Find one feature in your product that few people use and many find confusing. Apply the fence test: who uses it, why was it built, what happens if it goes. Write a recommendation (keep, fix, replace, remove with a path) and present it.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the state card and selector (a time you chose where to focus using data), the winners-and-losers table (a time you protected existing users when you changed something), and the sizing (a time you estimated impact and were honest about the range). Keep the numbers real, and keep what you did separate from what the team did.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c08-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>An existing product has a role, users, a funnel and a history; state them before you propose a change, and treat what you do not know as assumptions to check.</li>\n<li>Choose the area by reach, pain and headroom, and follow the gain downstream to the outcome you care about; the biggest raw gap is not always the best step.</li>\n<li>Offer different types of move (fix, add, remove, reposition), and test any removal by asking who uses it and why it exists.</li>\n<li>Size the gain with a visible chain, haircut it, give a range, and credit only the causal part of any correlation.</li>\n<li>Name who loses, count net and not gross, and set a guardrail, a counter metric and a condition for reversing.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c08-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Marty Cagan, <em>Inspired</em> (2nd edition, 2017), and <em>Empowered</em> (2020). How strong product teams choose problems and improve live products.</li>\n<li>Melissa Perri, <em>Escaping the Build Trap</em> (2018). Why teams add features instead of improving outcomes.</li>\n<li>Alistair Croll and Benjamin Yoskovitz, <em>Lean Analytics</em> (2013). Choosing the metric that matters at each stage of a product <code>[V: O'Reilly, 2013, ISBN 9781449335670]</code>.</li>\n<li>Sean Ellis and Morgan Brown, <em>Hacking Growth</em> (2017). A practical view of improving funnels through experiments.</li>\n<li>Steve Krug, <em>Don't Make Me Think</em> (revised edition, 2014). Short and practical on removing friction from interfaces.</li>\n<li>G. K. Chesterton, <em>The Thing</em> (1929), in the essay &quot;The Drift from Domesticity&quot;, for the passage on the fence that gives the fence test its name <code>[V: Chesterton, The Thing (1929), essay The Drift from Domesticity]</code>.</li>\n<li>Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky, &quot;Prospect Theory: An Analysis of Decision under Risk,&quot; <em>Econometrica</em> (1979), the source of the research on loss aversion <code>[V: Econometrica 47(2), 263 to 291, March 1979 (RePEc)]</code>.</li>\n<li>Twitter, &quot;Giving you more characters to express yourself&quot; (26 September 2017) and &quot;Tweeting Made Easier&quot; (7 November 2017), now hosted at blog.x.com/en_us/topics/product/2017/. The primary sources for CS-C08-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c09\"></span>\n<h2>C09 — New Markets and Underserved Users</h2>\n<span id=\"c09-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;How would you launch this product in a country where it does not exist yet?&quot; and &quot;Design something for people whom current products do not serve&quot; are product-sense questions with a twist. The product or the person is unfamiliar, and the interviewer wants to see whether you assume the world looks like your own. A candidate who copies a home-market product and translates it fails the question, however polished the rest of the answer is.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: new-market and underserved-user prompts appear in product-sense rounds at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Stripe and others, often as a variant of a design or improvement prompt (for example, &quot;design for users with limited connectivity&quot; or &quot;launch a wallet where cards are uncommon&quot;) <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Several of these companies serve people in many countries and with many abilities, which probably explains why the question recurs <code>Inferred</code>. On the job it is the work of a PM asked to take a product to a new country or to a group the product was not built for.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> The same five things as a design round (structure, user choice, problem choice, solutions, a decision with a measure), plus three that belong to this question <code>Inferred</code>: whether you identified what is different about the market and what you do not know, whether you found the binding constraint and designed to it, and whether you handled distribution, economics and responsibility, not only the interface.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n";
